<===> README.md
`math.sign()` is a LibSass extension (dart-sass has no `sign` in the
`sass:math` module), so these use `output-libsass.css`. It follows the
rules of the CSS `sign()` calculation: -1 or 1, and 0, -0 and NaN are
returned unchanged. It used to return the string "0.0" or "-0.0" for
every input.
<===>
================================================================================
<===> values/input.scss
@use 'sass:math';
a {
  positive: math.sign(3);
  negative: math.sign(-5.6);
  zero: math.sign(0);
  fuzzy: math.sign(0.000000000001);
  infinity: math.sign(math.div(-1, 0));
}
<===> values/output-libsass.css
a {
  positive: 1;
  negative: -1;
  zero: 0;
  fuzzy: 1;
  infinity: -1;
}
<===>
================================================================================
<===> is_number/input.scss
@use 'sass:math';
@use 'sass:meta';
a {
  type: meta.type-of(math.sign(-2));
  math: math.sign(-2) * 3;
}
<===> is_number/output-libsass.css
a {
  type: number;
  math: -3;
}
<===>
================================================================================
<===> signed_zero/input.scss
@use 'sass:math';
a {
  zero: math.div(1, math.sign(0));
  negative-zero: math.div(1, math.sign(-0.0));
}
<===> signed_zero/output-libsass.css
a {
  zero: calc(infinity);
  negative-zero: calc(-infinity);
}
<===>
================================================================================
<===> nan/input.scss
@use 'sass:math';
a {b: math.sign(math.div(0, 0))}
<===> nan/output-libsass.css
a {
  b: calc(NaN);
}
